# AuntieOS admin: local/build environment.
#
# WHAT DOES *NOT* BELONG HERE
# Every real server secret lives in Google Secret Manager and is bound per
# function via `secrets: [...]` in the function's options. That includes
# SMTP2GO_API_KEY, EMAIL_FROM, TWILIO_*, STRIPE_*, TRIBE_PIN_PEPPER,
# WEB_API_KEY, AUNTIE_OPERATOR_UIDS, SENTRY_DSN, MAPBOX_ACCESS_TOKEN and
# CLOUDINARY_*. Do not copy any of them into a .env file: a function reads the
# bound secret, not this file, so a value here would be silently ignored while
# looking authoritative.
#
# REMOVED 2026-07-23, do not re-add
# The Baserow block (BASEROW_URL/TOKEN/EMAIL/PASSWORD/JWT/REFRESH/DATABASE_ID
# and the five BASEROW_TABLE_* ids) and N8N_URL/N8N_TOKEN: both services were
# retired and the only readers left were root-level Python scripts that are
# themselves dead. AUNTIE_SERVER_HOST/PORT and AUNTIE_SHARED_SECRET went with
# them: they had zero consumers anywhere in the repo, in any language.

# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY / ANTHROPIC_MODEL were removed 2026-07-23 along with the 19
# root-level Baserow/n8n Python scripts that were their only readers. Every
# deployed Anthropic path uses Secret Manager: web/functions binds the key, and
# web/functions-python reads its own directory or its binding. Do not re-add
# them here.

# --- Web admin (Vite / React) ---
#
# WHERE THESE COME FROM, and it is no longer this file alone.
# Every VITE_* variable either web app reads is declared in
# scripts/client-secrets.mjs, and release step 0c fills it from Google Secret
# Manager before anything is built. This file is the LOCAL fallback: it is what
# your own `npm run dev` and `npm run build` use when nothing else supplies a
# value. Precedence, which is Vite's own:
#
#   1. process.env                     an explicit override; how CI passes a
#                                      repo secret into a preview build
#   2. .env.production.local           the release, from Secret Manager.
#                                      Generated, removed when it finishes, and
#                                      read only by a production build.
#   3. .env.local, .env                this file, filled in by you
#
# `node scripts/client-secrets.mjs --list` prints every variable, which Secret
# Manager secret backs it, and whether a release refuses without it.
#
# An earlier version of this file said these "cannot live in Secret Manager".
# That confused two things. The VALUE does ship inside the bundle every browser
# downloads. That part is true, and it is why only public client identifiers
# belong in a VITE_ variable. But `vite build` reads the value from the machine
# it runs on, and a release can perfectly well put it there. What that buys is
# not secrecy. It is one place to look, and a build that can be reproduced by
# someone who is not holding the original laptop.

# Sentry crash reporting for the React admin at auntie.tribetails.com.
# Secret Manager: ADMIN_WEB_SENTRY_DSN. A release REFUSES without it.
#
# The DSN belongs to the `auntieos-admin` project in the `tribetails` Sentry
# org (Settings > Client Keys / DSN), a DIFFERENT project from the portal's, so
# a different DSN. Public by design: it permits write-only event ingestion, and
# what bounds abuse is Sentry-side rate limits and inbound filters.
#
# Leave it empty locally and reporting is simply off (the app logs
# "Sentry disabled: no VITE_SENTRY_DSN" and runs normally). It was empty in
# PRODUCTION from 2026-07-23, which is why a two-hour CRITICAL error loop in
# notificationBatchSweep raised no alert at all. That is what makes it required
# for a release and not for you.
VITE_SENTRY_DSN=

# Tags events with a release so Sentry can group a regression against the deploy
# that introduced it.
#
# DERIVED, not stored: a release sets this to the commit it is shipping. Set it
# locally only if you want your own dev events tagged. A value maintained by
# hand in a .env is a value that names the last release someone remembered to
# edit it for.
VITE_SENTRY_RELEASE=

# reCAPTCHA Enterprise SITE key for admin App Check (#576).
# Secret Manager: ADMIN_WEB_APPCHECK_SITE_KEY. A release WARNS without it.
#
# A site key is a public identifier: it ships in the bundle and identifies which
# reCAPTCHA key the page is scoring against. The secret half never leaves
# Google. What binds it is the key's allowed-domains list.
#
# It warns rather than refuses because the key does not exist yet. Checked
# 2026-08-24: the only App Check reCAPTCHA key on auntieos-ttpc is
# `mytribe-appcheck-web`, whose allowed domains are the portal's, and O-3 D1
# forbids sharing one key between two assessment streams. Someone has to mint
# `auntieos-appcheck-web` for auntie.tribetails.com first. Until then the admin
# reports App Check `unconfigured` and says so on every boot.
VITE_ADMIN_APPCHECK_SITE_KEY=
